(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antibacterials-quiz"]={"title":"Antibacterials Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which beta-lactamase inhibitor is paired with piperacillin?","choices":["Tazobactam, marketed as Zosyn","Sulbactam, marketed as Unasyn","Clavulanate, marketed as Augmentin","Avibactam, marketed as Avycaz"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sulbactam pairs with ampicillin as Unasyn, and clavulanate with amoxicillin as Augmentin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which serious skin reactions do the aminopenicillins cause?","choices":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","Photosensitivity and permanent discoloration of the teeth","Tendonitis and rupture of the Achilles tendon","Red man syndrome during infusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These sit alongside hepatic dysfunction, Clostridium difficile infection, interstitial nephritis and haematologic effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What are aminoglycosides drug of choice for?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia and acute otitis media","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection","Clostridium difficile colitis","Febrile neutropenia, sepsis, and enterococcal synergy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with Enterococcus covered only at synergy dosing rather than as monotherapy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which group of five agents is bacteriostatic?","choices":["Erythromycin, tetracyclines, sulfonamides, trimethoprim, clindamycin","Penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, metronidazole","Erythromycin, penicillins, sulfonamides, vancomycin, fluoroquinolones","Tetracyclines, clindamycin, aminoglycosides, metronidazole, trimethoprim"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the five on the bacteriostatic side. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does ceftriaxone differ from other cephalosporins in renal impairment?","choices":["It must be dialysed off before each subsequent dose","It requires no dosage adjustment in renal insufficiency","It requires the largest dose reduction of any cephalosporin in renal insufficiency","It is contraindicated at any degree of renal impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is a useful contrast because the class as a whole is mostly renally eliminated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is metronidazole's mechanism of action?","choices":["It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase","It interacts with bacterial DNA to cause loss of helical structure and strand breakage","It inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Binds 50S and blocks transpeptidation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its activity spans Gram-positive and Gram-negative anaerobes plus parasites. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"When should a vancomycin trough be drawn?","choices":["At any point within the dosing interval, provided the exact time of the draw is recorded","15 to 30 minutes before the next scheduled dose, usually around the third or fourth dose","30 minutes after the end of the infusion","Immediately before the second dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drawing around the third or fourth dose is what makes the level reflect steady state. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which pharmacokinetic properties count under drug factors?","choices":["Bioavailability, protein binding, volume of distribution, and clearance","Bactericidal activity, time-dependent killing, and concentration-dependent killing","Absorption, distribution, penetration into body compartments, and elimination","Onset, peak, duration, and half-life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Penetration into compartments is called out separately, which is why central nervous system penetration recurs for individual classes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are carbapenems drug of choice for?","choices":["MRSA bacteraemia, endocarditis and osteomyelitis","Uncomplicated urinary tract infection and outpatient sinusitis","Atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma or Legionella","Resistant Gram-negatives, ESBL producers, meningitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Their resistance to hydrolysis by beta-lactamases is what makes them the answer to extended-spectrum producers. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Linezolid is drug of choice for what, and what does it miss?","choices":["Hospital and community MRSA, with no Gram-negative cover","Pseudomonal and resistant Gram-negative infection only","Anaerobic intra-abdominal infection; it does not cover aerobes","Atypical pneumonia; it does not cover Staphylococcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It also covers multidrug-resistant pneumococcus and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the tetracycline mechanism of action?","choices":["Channels that let potassium and magnesium leak out","Binds 16S RNA of the 30S subunit, blocking the A site","Binding the 50S subunit and blocking transpeptidation","Inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase and blocking folate synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the result is bacteriostatic and broad-spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What makes macrolide QT prolongation more dangerous?","choices":["Class Ia and III antiarrhythmics, electrolyte abnormalities","Iron, antacids, calcium or dairy taken at the same time","Large intravenous doses given in renal insufficiency","Use of the estolate salt in underlying liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each pushes repolarisation further, so the effects stack. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Why do high levels of a protein synthesis inhibitor harm the host?","choices":["At high levels it can reach the mammalian 80S ribosome","The drug is metabolized to a reactive intermediate that damages hepatocytes","Human cells lack the efflux pumps that protect bacteria from accumulation","Mitochondria use a 90S ribosome that binds these drugs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selectivity is a matter of degree, not an absolute barrier, which is why these agents have dose-related toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the polymyxin mechanism of action?","choices":["Binds 30S and blocks transfer RNA at the A site","Covalent inactivation of penicillin-binding proteins","A detergent action on the Gram-negative outer membrane","Inhibition of 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase in the cell wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why the spectrum is broad Gram-negative coverage — the target only exists there. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which infusion reaction is characteristic of vancomycin?","choices":["Serotonin syndrome when combined with a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor","Tendonitis and Achilles tendon rupture","Red man syndrome, along with fever, chills and phlebitis","Disulfiram-like reaction when combined with ethanol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity among the monitoring concerns. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which Bactrim interaction runs through CYP2C9 inhibition?","choices":["Reduced effectiveness of oral contraceptives through gut flora disruption","Serotonin syndrome with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors","Chelation reducing absorption of iron supplements","A significant increase in the international normalised ratio in patients on warfarin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Other interactions include raised phenytoin, rifampin and digoxin levels, reduced methotrexate clearance, and hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the activity profile of cefazolin and cephalexin?","choices":["Activity restricted to atypical organisms such as Legionella and Mycoplasma","Gram-positives without Enterococcus, some Gram-negative cover","Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas, plus Enterococcus","Broad coverage including anaerobes and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The absence of Enterococcus coverage is a recurring theme across the whole cephalosporin class. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin is preferred in neonatal sepsis, and which is restricted?","choices":["Ceftriaxone is preferred; cefotaxime cannot be used in the first 30 days of life","Ceftazidime is preferred; cefepime cannot be used in the first 30 days of life","Cefdinir is preferred; cefixime cannot be used in the first 30 days of life","Cefotaxime is preferred; ceftriaxone cannot be used in the first 30 days of life"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pairing is worth holding together, because the two agents are otherwise close substitutes. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How does concentration-dependent killing differ from time-dependent?","choices":["More drug kills more, against time above the level","Continuous infusion, against one large daily dose","Static, against cidal","Renal clearance, against hepatic metabolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. time above the minimal inhibitory concentration as 40 to 70 percent of the dosing interval for time-dependent agents. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is monitored for with imipenem?","choices":["Red man syndrome","Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol","Seizures","Tendon rupture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The monitoring list includes renal and hepatic function and complete blood count for bleeding, with seizures called out for imipenem in particular. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Aztreonam's spectrum resembles which class?","choices":["Glycopeptides","Nitroimidazoles","Aminoglycosides","Macrolides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both cover Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterobacteriaceae, with no Gram-positive or anaerobic activity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which cardiac risk do the fluoroquinolones carry?","choices":["Bradycardia and atrioventricular block","QT prolongation and torsades de pointes","Cardiomyopathy after prolonged therapy","Hypertensive crisis with tyramine foods"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The same cardiac risk macrolides and posaconazole carry. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone must not be used for urinary tract infection?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Levofloxacin","All three are equally suitable for urinary tract infection","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It also lacks Pseudomonas coverage, unlike levofloxacin, and needs no renal dose adjustment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What are Augmentin and Unasyn drug of choice for?","choices":["Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia","Clostridium difficile colitis, intra-abdominal abscess, and pseudomembranous colitis","Syphilis and gas gangrene","Skin and soft tissue infection, diabetic foot infection, and animal or human bites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The added Bacteroides and methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus coverage is what fits these polymicrobial sites. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the dual mechanism of action of the fluoroquinolones?","choices":["Inhibition of cell wall cross-linking and of beta-lactamase","Inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Inhibition of the 30S and 50S ribosomal subunits simultaneously","Sulfa at the reductase, trimethoprim upstream of it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The topoisomerase IV mechanism is poorly understood. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What is the vancomycin loading dose in a critically ill patient?","choices":["7 milligrams per kilogram once daily","1 gram regardless of weight","25 to 30 milligrams per kilogram, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams","15 to 20 milligrams per kilogram, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The loading dose exists to reach the target trough quickly rather than waiting through several maintenance doses. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are the polymyxin black box warnings?","choices":["Ototoxicity, red man syndrome, and phlebitis","Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, and neuromuscular blockade","Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and central nervous system effects","Thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome, and hypertensive crisis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the only warnings labels explicitly as black box, which is why they are worth holding separately from ordinary adverse effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which organisms do tetracyclines cover excellently?","choices":["Atypicals and animal-borne organisms","Pseudomonas, C. difficile, E. faecium, Acinetobacter","Enterococcus and Listeria monocytogenes only","Anaerobes exclusively, with no aerobic activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Poor coverage is specified for Pseudomonas and Clostridium difficile. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which infection does Bactrim both treat and prevent?","choices":["Mycobacterium avium complex","Clostridium difficile colitis","Lyme disease","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside urinary tract infection, bacterial prostatitis, orchitis and epididymitis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which cephalosporins cover Pseudomonas?","choices":["Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, and cefdinir","Ceftaroline, cefoxitin, and cefprozil","Ceftazidime, cefepime, and ceftolozane","Cefazolin, cephalexin, and cefuroxime"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antipseudomonal activity does not follow generation neatly, which is why these three are named specifically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"}]};
